Based on your conversation history, you've already explored several caching approaches. Since you want to keep it **simple and straightforward** and you're working with a weather app that has a **1,000 daily API call limit** and a goal of keeping the app **under 2.5MB** (lightweight, no heavy dependencies), here's a clean, simple caching system you can use:

## Simple In-Memory + localStorage Cache

```javascript
const CACHE_PREFIX = 'weather_';
const CACHE_TTL = 30 * 60 * 1000; // 30 minutes

class SimpleCache {
  constructor() {
    this.memory = {};
  }

  get(key) {
    // Check in-memory first (fastest)
    if (this.memory[key] && this.memory[key].expires > Date.now()) {
      return this.memory[key].data;
    }

    // Check localStorage (persists across sessions)
    const stored = localStorage.getItem(CACHE_PREFIX + key);
    if (stored) {
      const { data, expires } = JSON.parse(stored);
      if (expires > Date.now()) {
        this.memory[key] = { data, expires };
        return data;
      }
      // Expired — remove it
      localStorage.removeItem(CACHE_PREFIX + key);
    }

    return null;
  }

  set(key, data) {
    const entry = { data, expires: Date.now() + CACHE_TTL };
    this.memory[key] = entry;
    try {
      localStorage.setItem(CACHE_PREFIX + key, JSON.stringify(entry));
    } catch (e) {
      // localStorage full or unavailable — in-memory still works
      console.warn('Could not persist to localStorage:', e);
    }
  }

  clear() {
    this.memory = {};
    Object.keys(localStorage)
      .filter(k => k.startsWith(CACHE_PREFIX))
      .forEach(k => localStorage.removeItem(k));
  }
}

const cache = new SimpleCache();

async function fetchWeather(city) {
  const cached = cache.get(city);
  if (cached) {
    console.log(`Cache hit for ${city}`);
    return cached;
  }

  try {
    const response = await fetch(
      `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${city}&units=metric&appid=${API_KEY}`
    );
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
    const data = await response.json();
    cache.set(city, data);
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Error fetching weather for ${city}:`, error);
    return null;
  }
}
```

## Why This Works Well for Your Constraints

| Concern | How It's Handled |
|---|---|
| **Lightweight / no dependencies** | Pure JavaScript, no libraries |
| **1,000 daily call limit** | 30-min TTL means each city is fetched at most ~48×/day; most repeated lookups hit the cache |
| **App size < 2.5MB** | ~1KB of code, zero external packages |
| **Fast response (<300ms)** | In-memory hits are instant; localStorage hits are near-instant |
| **Persistence** | localStorage keeps data across page reloads and sessions |
| **Graceful degradation** | If localStorage is full/unavailable, in-memory cache still works |

## Optional: Add a Stale-While-Revalidate Fallback

If you want to serve slightly stale data when the API is down or rate-limited (instead of returning `null`), you can modify `get` to return expired data as a fallback:

```javascript
get(key, allowStale = false) {
  // ... existing fresh checks ...

  if (allowStale) {
    const stored = localStorage.getItem(CACHE_PREFIX + key);
    if (stored) {
      const { data } = JSON.parse(stored);
      return data; // serve stale data
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

Then in `fetchWeather`, if the API call fails, call `cache.get(city, true)` to return the last known data.

This keeps everything simple, dependency-free, and well within your size and API quota constraints.

