Based on the conversation history, here is a recommended setup for adding lazy loading to your Bootstrap 5.3.0 image gallery using `lazysizes` (v5.3.2), which aligns with your goal of keeping the bundle size under 100KB and ensuring smooth integration.

### 1. HTML Structure
Use Bootstrap’s grid system for layout. For each image, use the `lazyload` class and the `data-src` attribute for the actual image source. Use a small placeholder image for the `src` attribute to prevent layout shifts.

```html
<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-md-4 mb-4">
            <img class="w-100 lazyload" data-src="path/to/image1.jpg" src="placeholder.jpg" alt="Image 1">
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-4 mb-4">
            <img class="w-100 lazyload" data-src="path/to/image2.jpg" src="placeholder.jpg" alt="Image 2">
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-4 mb-4">
            <img class="w-100 lazyload" data-src="path/to/image3.jpg" src="placeholder.jpg" alt="Image 3">
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
```

### 2. Include Lazysizes
Include `lazysizes` via CDN to keep your bundle lightweight. It is recommended to load it asynchronously to avoid blocking page rendering.

```html
<!-- In <head> -->
<link rel="preload" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/lazysizes/5.3.2/lazysizes.min.js" as="script">

<!-- Before closing </body> -->
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/lazysizes/5.3.2/lazysizes.min.js" async></script>
```

### 3. JavaScript Configuration & Error Handling
Initialize `lazysizes` and add robust error handling to ensure a smooth user experience if an image fails to load.

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // Initialize lazysizes with custom classes
  lazysizes.config({
    loadingClass: 'ls-loading',
    loadedClass: 'ls-loaded',
    lazyClass: 'lazy',
    errorClass: 'ls-error'
  });

  // Revalidate to ensure all lazy elements are detected
  lazysizes.revalidate();

  // Add error handling for images
  const images = document.querySelectorAll('.lazyload');
  images.forEach((image) => {
    image.addEventListener('error', () => {
      console.error(`Failed to load ${image.getAttribute('data-src')}.`);
      // Set a fallback image
      image.src = 'fallback.jpg';
      image.classList.remove('ls-error');
    });
  });
});
```

### Key Performance & Integration Tips
*   **Placeholder Images:** Use a very small, low-resolution placeholder (e.g., 1x1 pixel or a blurred thumbnail) to minimize initial load time and prevent Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).
*   **SEO:** Always include descriptive `alt` attributes on your `<img>` tags.
*   **Bootstrap Compatibility:** `lazysizes` works independently of Bootstrap, so it integrates seamlessly with Bootstrap 5.3.0 components like cards and grids. Ensure your images have `w-100` or similar Bootstrap utility classes to maintain responsive sizing.
*   **Bundle Size:** `lazysizes` is very lightweight (~2KB gzipped), helping you stay well under your 100KB bundle size limit.

